.TH BTAR 1 "2026-10-25" "btar 1.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
btar \- tar archiver written in Bend
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B btar
.B \-c
.B \-f
.I ARCHIVE
.RI [ options ]
.I PATH ...
.br
.B btar
.B \-r
.RB | \-u
.B \-f
.I ARCHIVE
.RI [ options ]
.I PATH ...
.br
.B btar
.B \-t
.B \-f
.I ARCHIVE
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B btar
.B \-x
.B \-f
.I ARCHIVE
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B btar
creates, lists, extracts, appends to and updates tar archives. It reads v7, ustar, POSIX pax and the
GNU longname/longlink and base-256 extensions; it writes ustar or POSIX pax.
gzip input is detected automatically and gzip output is produced with
.BR \-z .
The pure format/policy core is written in Bend and covered by machine-checked
laws; a small audited C effect layer performs syscalls and data copying.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-t ", " \-x
Create, list or extract an archive.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-append
Append the operands to an existing
.I uncompressed
archive (created if absent). The existing entries are kept byte for byte; the new ones are
written over the end-of-archive blocks, followed by a fresh trailer. The archive is validated
first: a corrupt, truncated or compressed archive is refused with exit status 2 and left
untouched. Hardlinks are recorded only between appended files.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-update
Like
.BR \-r ,
but append only entries that are not yet in the archive or whose mtime (seconds) is newer than
every archived copy of the same name. Directories already in the archive are never re-added.
.B \-r
and
.B \-u
cannot be combined with
.BR \-z ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-t ,
.B \-x
or each other.
.TP
.BI \-f " ARCHIVE"
Archive file (required).
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-gzip
gzip mode. Reading is auto-detected from the gzip magic; with
.B \-c
the output archive is gzip-compressed (128 KiB segments compressed in parallel on all cores;
the output does not depend on the thread count).
.TP
.BI \-C " DIR"
Change to DIR before processing members/create operands.
.TP
.BR \-v
Verbose: print member names.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-preserve\-permissions
Preserve permission bits (setuid/setgid are still cleared when not root).
.TP
.BI \-\-strip\-components= N
Extraction: drop the first N path components.
.TP
.BR \-O
Extraction: write regular-file payloads to stdout.
.TP
.BI \-\-one\-top\-level[= DIR ]
Extraction: place all members under an (optional) top-level directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-format= ustar | pax
Create: require ustar (fail if a member does not fit) or prefer pax headers.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude= PATTERN
Skip members/files whose stored name matches the wildcard PATTERN (repeatable; also
.BI \-\-exclude " PATTERN" ).
GNU semantics: the pattern is not anchored (it may match after any
.BR / ),
.BR * ,
.B ?
and
.B [...]
also match
.BR / ,
and a matching directory excludes its contents. Works with create, append, update, list and extract.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude\-from= FILE ", " \-X " FILE"
Read patterns from FILE, one per line (trailing whitespace stripped, empty lines ignored).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-recursion
Create/append/update: archive a directory operand as a directory entry only.
.TP
.BR \-\-sort=name
Create: sort each directory's entries by raw name bytes (deterministic output).
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-V
Print the version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Completed with warnings (for example unsupported special entries were skipped).
.TP
.B 2
Fatal error (bad archive, unsafe member, I/O or compression failure, limit exceeded).
.SH SECURITY
Extraction treats the archive as untrusted: absolute paths and
.B ..
components are rejected; symlinks are never followed during extraction
(openat2 with RESOLVE_BENEATH and RESOLVE_NO_SYMLINKS plus O_NOFOLLOW); device,
FIFO and special entries are refused; hardlinks must target an
already-extracted regular file under the destination root; member sizes,
counts, names and headers are bounded.
.SH LIMITS
Linux x86-64; members/archives are limited to 4 GiB. Append and update work on uncompressed
archives only (GNU tar has the same restriction) and do not support
.BR \-A ", " \-\-delete ", " \-d ,
incremental or multi-volume archives. gzip is streamed: there is no limit on the
size of a compressed archive, and a multi-GiB
.B .tar.gz
is listed or extracted in a few tens of MiB of memory. Compression uses every CPU core by default;
memory grows with the number of threads (about 100 MiB at 24 threads, 35 MiB at one). Pass
.B +RTS \-\-threads
.I N
.B \-RTS
to bound it. Archive names
.B \-
(stdin/stdout) are not supported.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BENDRTS
Extra runtime options for the Bend runtime (for example
.BR "\-\-threads 4" ).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tar (1),
.BR gzip (1)
